  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the minimum probability of any outcome in the lottery (i.e., the smallest probability assigned to a single outcome), and prefers the lottery with the lower such value. In contrast, the individual focuses on the probability of winning (i.e., the chance of getting a positive amount), which corresponds to the probability of the non-zero outcome. For Lottery A, this is 25%, for Lottery B, 20%. The individual values increasing this 'chance of success' — meaning they would prefer higher probabilities of winning, so they would prefer A over B. However, the decision reason prefers B because B has a lower minimum probability (20% vs 25%). The individual’s consideration of 'chance to win' is conceptually opposite to the logic of minimizing the smallest probability. Moreover, the justification in the report emphasizes increasing success probability, which contradicts choosing based on minimizing a probability. Therefore, the individual’s reasoning is not aligned with the given decision reason. The information considered (probability of winning) is different from that required by the reason (minimum probability of any outcome), and the decision rule (favor higher chance of gain) opposes the reason’s rule (favor lower minimum probability).",
